{"records":[{"id":"vjs8hd9xwj","title":"16S ribosomal RNA gene sequencing - Mice with or without antibiotics","description":"We assessed the fecal gut microbiome composition of 18 wild-type mice (8 controls and 10 antibiotics treated) using the QIAseq 16S/ITS screening panel (QIAGEN), sequenced on a MiSeq (Illumina). Briefly, samples were thawed and total DNA of 1 or 2 stool pellets was extracted with a QIAamp PowerFecal Pro DNA Kit (QIAGEN). Sequencing libraries were generated with the QIAseq 16S/ITS Screening Panels kit. For each sample, 1 ng of total DNA was amplified with each of the three Screening Panel pools and subsequently combined and indexed. The indexed libraries were pooled equimolarly, and 15pM were loaded and sequenced PE 276 on a MiSeq v3 600-cycle kit (Illumina). Taxonomy was assigned using the IDTAXA classifier (DECIPHER package) with the GTDB-RS89 reference database. The processed data were integrated into a phyloseq object for further analysis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-04-14T15:21:59.109Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vjs8hd9xwj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vjs8hd9xwj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jessica","last_name":"Rebeaud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Solenne","last_name":"Vigne","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lena","last_name":"Siewert","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tim","last_name":"Roloff","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Adrian","last_name":"Egli","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anne-Katrin","last_name":"Proebstel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Caroline","last_name":"Pot","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mouse","Antibiotics","Gut Microbiota","Experimental Autoimmune Encephalomyelitis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"sdxtcfpk4y","title":"Mouse stool sequencing before and after EAE","description":"We assessed the fecal gut microbiome composition of 18 wild-type mice (16 controls and 17 antibiotics treated) before and after EAE adoptive transfer using the QIAseq 16S/ITS screening panel (QIAGEN), sequenced on a MiSeq (Illumina). Briefly, samples were thawed and total DNA of 1 or 2 stool pellets was extracted with a QIAamp PowerFecal Pro DNA Kit (QIAGEN). Sequencing libraries were generated with the QIAseq 16S/ITS Screening Panels kit. For each sample, 1 ng of total DNA was amplified with each of the three Screening Panel pools and subsequently combined and indexed. The indexed libraries were pooled equimolarly, and 15pM were loaded and sequenced PE 276 on a MiSeq v3 600-cycle kit (Illumina). Taxonomy was assigned using the IDTAXA classifier (DECIPHER package) with the GTDB-RS89 reference database. The processed data were integrated into a phyloseq object for further analysis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-15T18:52:28.465Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/sdxtcfpk4y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/sdxtcfpk4y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jessica","last_name":"Rebeaud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Caroline","last_name":"Pot","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["rRNA Analysis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dv7t7mrt3j","title":"Target and Non-target Discovery Screening of Poly-and Per-Fluoroalkyl Substances (PFASs) in Environmental Biota-Samples","description":"There are workflow and databases for non-target screening PFASs in complex environmental biota- samples using ultra-high performance liquid chromatography and quadrupole- orbitrap high-resolution mass spectrometer with software of Compound Discoverer. \nUser can download these databases and import them into Mass Lists, Spectral Libraries and Compound Classes in Lists & Library of Compound Discoverer software, respectively, and then use the workflow to make non-target screening for PFASs. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-12-22T15:09:21.659Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dv7t7mrt3j","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dv7t7mrt3j"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Shaogang","last_name":"Chu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Robert","last_name":"Letcher","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Liquid Chromatography Mass Spectrometry","Per- and Polyfluoroalkyl Substances"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"65pfcj4j4m","title":"Protocol of CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens for identifying ferroptosis regulators","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent programmed cell death triggered by excessive lipid peroxidation, has shown promising therapeutic potentials in human diseases. Here, we describe a protocol of a CRISPR-Cas9 loss-of-function screen to identify regulators in response to different inducers of ferroptosis. We emphasize the steps of library amplification, drug treatment, high-throughput sequencing preparation, and bioinformatics analysis using Model-based Analysis of Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 Knockout (MAGeCK). We also present a method to discover the regulators of ferroptosis and verify the potential targets efficiently.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-24T01:46:48.793Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65pfcj4j4m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65pfcj4j4m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dzszp4pbc7","title":"Distinct Immunological Features Compared to Lichen Planus and Oral Lichen Planus","description":"Single Cell 3' libraries were prepared using standard Illumina paired-end constructs, which include the P5 and P7 adaptors. The libraries incorporated a 16 bp 10x Barcode and a 12 bp Unique Molecular Identifier (UMI) within Read 1. The cDNA fragments in the 3' Gene Expression libraries were sequenced using Read 2, while the DNA from Cell Multiplexing Oligo Feature Barcodes in the Cell Multiplexing libraries was sequenced using Read 2N. The libraries were subjected to paired-end sequencing on an Illumina platform. Sample indexing was achieved using i7 and i5 index sequences. The following standard Illumina sequencing primer sites were utilized: for the 3' Gene Expression libraries, TruSeq Read 1 and TruSeq Read 2 primers were employed; for the Cell Multiplexing libraries, Nextera Read 1 and Nextera Read 2 primers were used. This method ensured accurate and efficient sequencing of both gene expression and multiplexing features, facilitating comprehensive single-cell analysis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-10-17T10:08:11.431Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dzszp4pbc7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dzszp4pbc7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Dong Min","last_name":"Lim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Do Yeon","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hye-Min","last_name":"Ju","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sung-Hee","last_name":"Jeong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Soo-Min","last_name":"Ok","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hae Ryoun","last_name":"Park","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yun Hak","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/00fdzyk40","name":"Korea Health Industry Development Institute","grant_id":"HI22C1377"},{"id":"https://ror.org/013aysd81","name":"National Research Foundation of Korea","grant_id":"NRF-2018R1A5A2023879 "},{"id":"https://ror.org/013aysd81","name":"National Research Foundation of Korea","grant_id":"RS-2023-00207946"}],"categories":["Bioinformatics","Multiplexing","Single-Cell RNA Sequencing","Single-Cell Transcriptomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xf8rkc6xrb","title":"Raw, not cropped supporting Western blot files for: Dankert J. ... Pagano M.  FEM1 proteins are ancient regulators of SLBP degradation.  Cell Cycle 16:556-564, 2017 ","description":"Raw, not cropped supporting Western blot files for: Dankert J.F., Pagan J., Starostina N., Kipreos E., and Pagano M.  FEM1 proteins are ancient regulators of SLBP degradation.  Cell Cycle 16:556-564, 2017.  PMID:28118078, PMCID: PMC5384579, DOI: 10.1080/15384101.2017.1284715.  ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-10-15T23:18:42.691Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xf8rkc6xrb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xf8rkc6xrb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Michele","last_name":"Pagano","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vd9wvc9fp7","title":"Peripheral immune-cell changes during dinutuximab beta-based chemoimmunotherapy in a single patient with high-risk neuroblastoma: a longitudinal single-cell analysis.","description":"This dataset contains single-cell RNA sequencing data and the analysis code supporting the study “Peripheral immune-cell changes during dinutuximab beta-based chemoimmunotherapy in a single patient with high-risk neuroblastoma: a longitudinal single-cell analysis.”  \nPeripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) were collected from one participant enrolled in the ChIm-NB-PL clinical trial (EudraCT 2021-003832-96; ClinicalTrials.gov NCT05272371) at two longitudinal time points: immediately before initiation of dinutuximab beta-based chemoimmunotherapy (Sample 1) and 19 days after completion of the fifth treatment cycle (Sample 2).  \nSingle-cell gene-expression libraries were generated using the 10x Genomics Chromium platform and processed with Cell Ranger. The repository contains the filtered feature-barcode HDF5 matrices for Sample 1 and Sample 2, together with the R analysis script used for quality control, doublet removal, normalization, integration, cell-type annotation, ambient-RNA correction, differential-expression analysis, pathway enrichment, transcription-factor activity inference, per-cell signature scoring, NK-cell phenotyping and generation of manuscript figures.  \nThe deposited files are:  \n- Sample1_filtered_feature_bc_matrix.h5 – Cell Ranger filtered feature-barcode matrix for the pre-treatment sample\n- Sample2_filtered_feature_bc_matrix.h5 – Cell Ranger filtered feature-barcode matrix for the post-treatment sample\n- NBL_scRNAseq_script.R – complete analysis pipeline used in the study\nThe dataset represents a paired longitudinal analysis from a single patient and should be interpreted accordingly.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-25T15:20:19.184Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vd9wvc9fp7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vd9wvc9fp7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Marcin","last_name":"Czepiel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Karolina ","last_name":"Bukowska-Strakova","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aleksandra ","last_name":"Wieczorek","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Przemysław","last_name":"Błyszczuk","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Walentyna","last_name":"Balwierz","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/026nedj88","name":"Agencja Badań Medycznych","grant_id":"2020/ABM/01/00055"}],"categories":["Neuroblastoma","Single-Cell Transcriptomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fn95vr46kh","title":"Pro-apoptotic activity of Apis mellifera royal jelly hydrophilic fractions","description":"Apis mellifera royal jelly was separated into high and low molecular weight hydrophilic fractions and their antiproliferative activity against a highly metastatic human colorectal cancer cell line HCT-116 and a non-cancerous 3T3-L1 cell line was assayed using the MTT assay. The pro-apoptotic activity of the high molecular weight protein fraction was further assayed using various apoptotic markers and cell cycle analysis studies by flow cytometry. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-11-08T03:41:52.8Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fn95vr46kh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fn95vr46kh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ashutosh","last_name":"Acharya","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Flow Cytometry","Antiproliferative Activity","Sodium Dodecyl Sulfate Polyacrylamide Gel Electrophoresis","MTT Assay"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hctkwwzx5z","title":"Benchmarking AI Workflows for Hit Detection in High-Content Screening","description":"The data set contains Python scripts, raw and processed data to benchmark hit detection in high-content screening.\nThe raw data contains the extracted numerical features from high content images of 641 validated, highly selective pharmaceutically relevant inhibitors for over 123 targets. There were 979 features extracted using Perkin Elmer software Columbus (2.9.1532). In addition, two further raw data sets are available that were used to validate the machine learning models.\nThese contain staurosporines in different concentrations or compounds that effect the cell cycle. The processed data were cleaned using an outlier test and Z-score transformed. The processing of the data is also available as a Python script and can be followed there.\nThe other Python scripts contain the benchmarking of AI workflows for hit detection. Various machine learning models were tested, ranging from classic classifiers from the Scikit-Learn library over a partial least square regression to simple neural networks. To deal with unknown patterns in the hit detection, novelty detection was also tested.\nIn addition, the used packages or environments with which the benchmarking was carried out are included.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-09-15T14:37:09.094Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hctkwwzx5z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hctkwwzx5z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Erwin","last_name":"Kupczyk","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Machine Learning","Deep Learning","Classification (Machine Learning)","High-Content Screening"],"journal_issn":["20010370"]},{"id":"bs54ny7ns2","title":"Örd et al 2025 Supplementary Data and Code","description":"Supplementary Data and Code accompanying manuscript by M. Örd et al entitled “High-throughput investigation of cyclin docking interactions reveals the complexity of motif binding determinants”.\n\nSupplementary Data 1\nDescription: Experimental data for the cell cycle protein peptide tiling SIMBA pooled screens.\n\nSupplementary Data 2\nDescription: The cyclin binding motifs identified in the cell cycle protein peptide tiling SIMBA experiment.\n\nSupplementary Data 3\nDescription: Experimental data for mutational scanning SIMBA pooled screens.\n\nSupplementary Data 4\nDescription: Median SIMBA scores and p-values for mutational scanning SIMBA experiments.\n\nSupplementary Data 5\nDescription: DMS data from two distinct libraries (library 1 and library 2) that were tested independently.\n\nSupplementary Data 6\nDescription: Combined set of cyclin binding motifs from peptide tiling and mutational scanning SIMBA experiments.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-25T15:01:04.527Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bs54ny7ns2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bs54ny7ns2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mihkel","last_name":"Örd","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Cell Cycle","Cyclin-Dependent Kinase","Protein-Protein Interaction","High-Throughput Screening"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":112409,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":152,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":112409,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":111952,"restricted":54,"embargoed":403},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1831},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":577},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":406},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":354},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":336},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Científico e Tecnológico","count":336},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":330},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":304},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":215},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":186}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":110082},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1716},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":146},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":111},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":65},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":61},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":44},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":35},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":25},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":24}],"top_